# `committed` returning the default instead of reading the cursor.
#
# Every assertion on `committed()` in the crate, one test and two doctests,
# expected `Seq::ZERO`, so a method that returned the default and never touched
# the consumer cursor satisfied all three. `Consumer::position()`, the same
# cursor by another name, was asserted once and also only at zero.
#
# This is the one-polarity rule (readme.md) applied to a quantity rather than a
# predicate. A watermark asserted only where it has not moved is
# indistinguishable from one that cannot move. The detector now brackets a
# drain, zero before and `Seq( 1 )` after, so the assertion straddles the
# change.

id: S11
crate: ring_mpsc
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-08-30
round-failures: 1
detector: a_second_drain_sees_what_was_published_after_the_first

--- from
  pub fn committed( &self ) -> Seq
  {
    self.consumer_cursor().load( GATING )
--- to
